<article class="env-lemma" id="00IQ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00IQ">Lemma <span data-tag="00IQ">10.51.5</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian ring. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. Let $M$ be a finite $R$-module. Let $N = \bigcap _ n I^ n M$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
For every prime $\mathfrak p$, $I \subset \mathfrak p$ there exists a $f \in R$, $f \not\in \mathfrak p$ such that $N_ f = 0$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $I$ is contained in the Jacobson radical of $R$, then $N = 0$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Proof of (1). Let $x_1, \ldots , x_ n$ be generators for the module $N$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00IK" data-tag="00IK">10.51.1</a>. For every prime $\mathfrak p$, $I \subset \mathfrak p$ we see that the image of $N$ in the localization $M_{\mathfrak p}$ is zero, by Lemma <a href="/tag/00IP" data-tag="00IP">10.51.4</a>. Hence we can find $g_ i \in R$, $g_ i \not\in \mathfrak p$ such that $x_ i$ maps to zero in $N_{g_ i}$. Thus $N_{g_1g_2\ldots g_ n} = 0$. 
    <p>
      Part (2) follows from (1) and Lemma <a href="/tag/00HN" data-tag="00HN">10.23.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>